.TH VM_OBJECT 9 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Developer's Manual"
.SH NAME
vm_object \- virtual memory backing store abstraction
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/vm/vm_object.h>
.LP
.BI "vm_object_t *vm_object_allocate(vm_object_type_t " type ", size_t " size ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_object_reference(vm_object_t *" object ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_object_deallocate(vm_object_t *" object ");"
.br
.BI "vm_object_t *vm_object_shadow(vm_object_t *" source ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_object_add_page(vm_object_t *" object ", vm_page_t *" page ");"
.br
.BI "void vm_object_remove_page(vm_object_t *" object ", vm_page_t *" page ");"
.br
.BI "vm_page_t *vm_object_lookup_page(vm_object_t *" object ", uint64_t " pindex ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B vm_object
subsystem manages abstract data sources for virtual memory. Objects can be anonymous (zero-filled), file-backed (vnode), or physical device memory.
.SS Object Types
.IP \[bu] 2
.B VM_OBJ_TYPE_DEFAULT
\- Anonymous memory (SWAP/RAM).
.IP \[bu]
.B VM_OBJ_TYPE_VNODE
\- File-backed memory.
.IP \[bu]
.B VM_OBJ_TYPE_DEVICE
\- Memory mapped I/O.
.SS Shadow Objects
Shadow objects implement Copy-on-Write (COW). A shadow object points to a backing object. Pages present in the shadow overlay the backing object; missing pages fall back to the backing object.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.B vm_object_allocate
creates a new object of the given type and size.
.PP
.B vm_object_reference
increments the reference count.
.PP
.B vm_object_deallocate
decrements the reference count and frees the object if zero.
.PP
.B vm_object_shadow
creates a new shadow object pointing to
.IR source .
.PP
.B vm_object_lookup_page
finds a resident page at the given index.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vm_map (9),
.BR vm_page (9)
